1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,080
Watch out! I'm about to blow your mind, bro. Businesses want you to buy stuff.

2
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:12,640
I know. This is shocking news. But perhaps even more shocking is that online,

3
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:17,840
businesses drive to get you to open your wallets can often involve quite a bit of subterfuge known

4
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:24,720
as dark patterns. Surprisingly, this isn't the name of a metal band. Rather, it's a tactic

5
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:29,680
designed to influence you somehow, usually into buying a product or service you might not really

6
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:34,240
want to sign up for incessant marketing emails or simply to stop you from doing something the

7
00:00:34,240 --> 00:00:39,760
business may not want you to do. And dark patterns aren't one size fits all. Just like a Stephen

8
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:46,880
King monster, a dark pattern can take many forms. One classic example is how hard it often is to

9
00:00:46,880 --> 00:00:52,000
find a phone number to talk to a real human being when you need customer service. Websites often

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:56,160
instead direct you to fill out a form or text chat with a representative. If a phone number

11
00:00:56,160 --> 00:01:01,440
even exists, it's often buried in a subtle location that you have to click multiple links to find.

12
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:05,360
A related tactic is how hard some services make it to close your account.

13
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:10,160
You've probably seen how companies will bury the unsubscribe link on an email at the bottom in

14
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:16,480
tiny font. But this is small potatoes compared to trying to straight up cancel a membership.

15
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:21,040
Typically, bringing up a settings menu and clicking close your account isn't good enough.

16
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:25,200
Sites will either entice you to stay with a massive in your face offer to prevent you from

17
00:01:25,200 --> 00:01:30,880
canceling a subscription with the actual cancel link presented in a much smaller size. Or they'll

18
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:36,320
make it insanely hard to find the actual delete your account button. Amazon is notorious for this,

19
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:41,360
and many other things. The latter is a good example of what designers call a roach motel.

20
00:01:41,360 --> 00:01:46,320
Easy to sign up for an account and get in, but hard to leave. And while you might not end up dead,

21
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:52,000
like an actual roach, it makes life super annoying. And may even result in a recurring charge in

22
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,560
your card if you don't deactivate your account in time. But even if burying the link you actually

23
00:01:56,560 --> 00:02:02,240
want to click isn't a tactic a certain company is employing, there are other gotchas to watch out for.

24
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:07,360
One is how these sites will use UI elements to nudge the user in a certain direction.

25
00:02:07,360 --> 00:02:12,560
For example, making a buy tokens or keep my subscription button a bright color,

26
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:15,680
or at least a color the user recognizes from other parts of the service.

27
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:19,440
This can also be done in a less subtle way, called Confirm Shaming.

28
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:25,200
Wait, are we on Twitter? You see this a lot when you visit a retailer or travel site,

29
00:02:25,200 --> 00:02:29,920
and get a pop-up to sign up for a newsletter promising discounts and other exclusive offers.

30
00:02:29,920 --> 00:02:33,920
If you're not interested in getting emails, bam, you can't just click on an X button,

31
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:38,320
or a button that says close. Instead, you have to click a small link, saying something to the

32
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:43,840
effective, no thanks, I'd rather pay full price, or some other condescending nonsense.

33
00:02:43,840 --> 00:02:48,640
But possibly the worst form of Dark Pattern is the one that actively requires you to click an

34
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:54,160
extra button to opt out of something. This can be anything from how tedious some sites make it to

35
00:02:54,160 --> 00:03:01,760
reject unnecessary cookies. Yes, to having a box to install some unwanted antivirus program,

36
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:05,760
or unwanted toolbar pre-checked for you when you're trying to install something else

37
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:10,400
completely unrelated. By the time you've clicked through reflexively, congratulations!

38
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:15,200
You're now the proud owner of a trip protection package, and probably some malware.

39
00:03:15,200 --> 00:03:18,560
Of course, these aren't the only kinds of dark patterns floating around out there,

40
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:24,160
so while it's a pain, be sure to take a few extra seconds to read before you click.

41
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:27,360
You don't want to end up in a position where you agree to have your robot vacuum

42
00:03:27,360 --> 00:03:31,520
suck up all your loose change and hold it ransom. It happened to me.

